<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Mecklenburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1167 CE – 1348 CE · capital Wismar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Christian lordship founded by the Obotrite prince Pribislav, whose dynasty ruled Mecklenburg from 1167 to 1918.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion's</a> campaigns destroyed Obotrite power and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niklot">Prince Niklot</a> in 1160, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pribislav-of-mecklenburg">Pribislav</a> accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> overlordship. In 1167 Henry restored most of his father's lands to him, except the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-schwerin">County of Schwerin</a>. Pribislav's descendants took their title from the old stronghold of Mecklenburg near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/swedish-wismar">Wismar</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lords encouraged German settlement, founding towns such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-rostock">Rostock</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/swedish-wismar">Wismar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a>, several of which joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doberan-abbey">Doberan Abbey</a>, founded in 1171, became the family burial place. After 1229 the lands divided into Mecklenburg, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-werle">Werle</a>, Rostock and Parchim-Richenberg, each with its own branch.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1348 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-duke-of-mecklenburg">Albert II of Mecklenburg</a> to duke. His son Albert became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1364. Mecklenburg remained ruled by the same dynasty, divided into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg-Schwerin</a> and Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a>, until 1918, the only Slavic-origin ruling house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1167 CE – 1348 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.93%;width:3.60%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1167 CE – 1348 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wismar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pribislav, Henry Borwin I, Albert II of Mecklenburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German, Polabian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mecklenburg</dd></dl></aside></div>
